  "answer": "The acid-and-leaf experiment is designed to demonstrate how the stomach is protected from its own acid. In this experiment, two similar green leaves are taken. One leaf is coated with petroleum jelly or grease, while the other is left untreated. A weak acid is then applied to both leaves. After some time, it is observed that the leaf without petroleum jelly is affected by the acid, while the leaf coated with petroleum jelly remains unaffected. This is because the petroleum jelly acts like the mucus lining in the stomach, which protects the stomach walls from the corrosive effects of stomach acid. The mucus secreted by cells in the stomach walls forms a thin lining that counteracts the acid, similar to how petroleum jelly protects the leaf from the acid.",
